# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pnpm 12 reads its install settings from pnpm-workspace.yaml and ignores them
# here. The supply-chain policy (C-12.3) is there: ignoreScripts, frozenLockfile,
# saveExact and strictPeerDependencies, each with its reason. engine-strict,
# which this file used to set, is not carried over (the reason is there too).
#
# What stays here is what a tool still reads from .npmrc: pnpm's registry and auth
# entries (none are set) and the one setting below, which is for npm. pr.yml's
# path filters name this file, and scripts/tests/test_pnpm_effective_policy.py
# fails on any other setting here that pnpm ignores.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# For npm, not pnpm: npm run at the repo root, for example an accidental `npm install`,
# reads ignore-scripts from here. npm does not read this file in global mode (`-g`) or
# inside a workspace package. pnpm 12 reads ignoreScripts from pnpm-workspace.yaml and
# ignores this line.
ignore-scripts=true
#
# E02-T07: the root package's own `prepare` script (`husky`) installs the git
# hooks under .husky/. `pnpm install` runs no lifecycle script (ignoreScripts),
# so contributors run it explicitly, once per clone or worktree
# (reviewed in E02-X01):
#   pnpm run prepare
# It is a single, code-owned, checked-in script (`husky`, which only writes a
# `core.hooksPath` pointer and copies the committed `.husky/*` files: no
# network fetch, no arbitrary code execution). This is documented in
# CONTRIBUTING.md's setup steps. No other package may rely on
# `prepare`/`postinstall` without the same review + allowlist entry.
